<div class="container">
<h2>2025</h2>
<h3>September 11, 2025</h3>
<p>This newly developed metadata template (content standard) supports the management of ocean carbon, ocean acidification, and marine Carbon Dioxide Removal (mCDR) data. Its development benefited greatly from the Halifax Workshop held on September 26, 2024. The template builds on the original ocean acidification metadata template developed by Jiang et al. (2015) and was further enhanced through input from the international data management workshop in Venice, Italy (May 7–8, 2024; Jiang et al., 2024). The creation of this template was made possible through the contributions of many scientists from the ocean carbon, ocean acidification, and mCDR research communities, particularly those who participated in these workshops. We thank the Carbon To Sea Initiative and the NOAA Ocean Acidification Program (OAP) for funding this development.</p>
<p><strong>Citation:&nbsp;</strong><br>Jiang, L.-Q., Long, J., Tamsitt, V., Keller, D., Madlener, A., and Polnyi, I. (2025). A metadata template for ocean carbon, ocean acidification, and mCDR data management (Version 4.5). Zenodo. <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17102190">https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17102190</a>.</p>
<p>For reference, check out this paper describing Version 1.0 of the metadata template: Jiang, L.-Q., O'Connor, S. A., Arzayus, K. M., and Parsons, A. R.: A metadata template for ocean acidification data, Earth Syst. Sci. Data, 7, 117–125, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-7-117-2015">https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-7-117-2015</a>, 2015.</p>
<h2>2024</h2>
<h3>July 24, 2024</h3>
<p>A new set of coastal climatologies and atlases for ten ocean acidification indicators along the North American coasts have been developed. This marks the first time the World Ocean Atlas (WOA) technology has been applied to ocean carbon variables. The coastal ocean, home to approximately 90% of the world’s fisheries yields, is now represented in a new data product that illustrates ocean acidification (OA) status in U.S. coastal waters across 14 standardized depth layers, from the surface down to 500 meters. This product provides a benchmark for tracking future changes and will underpin the next regional OA vulnerability report, becoming an essential tool for the blue economy.&nbsp;</p>
<h4>Citation</h4>
<p>Jiang, L.-Q., Boyer, T. P., Paver, C. R., Yoo, H., Reagan, J. R., Alin, S. R., Barbero, L., Carter, B. R., Feely, R. A., &amp; Wanninkhof, R. (2024). Climatological distribution of ocean acidification variables along the North American ocean margins. <em>Earth System Science Data</em>, 16(7), 3383–3390. <a href="https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-16-3383-2024">https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-16-3383-2024</a>.</p>
<h3>July 4, 2024</h3>
<p>In a recent publication in the journal Scientific Data, OCADS Scientists Dr. LiQing Jiang, and Dr. Hyelim Yoo, as well as colleagues from the University of Washington and NOAA/NESDIS National Centers for Environmental Information produced mapped monthly data products of surface OA indicators from 1998 to 2022 on a 0.25° by 0.25° spatial grid for eleven Large Marine Ecosystems (LMEs) in the U.S. These data products, referred to as random forest regression LMEs (RFR-LMEs), were constructed using observations from the Surface Ocean CO2 Atlas, co-located surface ocean properties, and two types of machine-learning algorithms.</p>
<h4>Citation</h4>
<p>Sharp, J. D., Jiang, L.-Q., Carter, B. R., Lavin, P. D., Yoo, H., &amp; Cross, S. L. (2024). A mapped dataset of surface ocean acidification indicators in large marine ecosystems of the United States. <em>Scientific Data</em>, 11(1). <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-024-03530-7">https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-024-03530-7</a>.</p>
</div>
</div>

                    </div>
